// A const generic parameter carrying a bracketed RANGE, on a type used in a
// machine's attached data, makes the machine unplannable — the same phase and
// plausibly the same limitation as
// `tests/omega/pending/float/float_range_attached_field`.
//
//   data Buf<const N: u64> { items: [i32; N]; }               checks
//   data Buf<const N: u64> { items: [i32 in Wrapping; N]; }   checks
//   data Buf<const N: u64[1..=..]> { items: [i32; N]; }       OMITTED (this)
//   data Buf<const N: u64[1..=..]> { items: [i32 in Wrapping; N]; }  OMITTED
//
// all at `state graph: state signature: parameter signature: attached data
// shape`.
//
// The ELEMENT POLICY is ruled out and that is worth stating, because this
// fixture's origin `pass/generics/runtime_const_container_methods_exit`
// declares `items: [i32 in Wrapping; N]` and reads exactly like the
// member-policy family pinned elsewhere. It is not: the policy element checks
// on its own, and the plain element with a ranged const parameter omits.
//
// PAIRED, not merged. The other four fixtures at this phase carry a float
// field with a bracketed range; this one carries no float at all. Both
// triggers are a bracketed RANGE that the attached shape has to represent,
// which is the natural hypothesis for one underlying limitation in
// `shapes.add_attached_application` — but an INTEGER field range
// (`value: i32 [0..=10]`) CHECKS, so "ranges in the attached shape" is too
// broad as stated. Settle what distinguishes the admitted integer-field range
// from these two before repairing either.
use omega_language_std::console;
use omega::language::core::binding;

data Buffer<const Capacity: u64[1..=64]> {
    items: [i32; Capacity];
}

data Main {
    console: Binding<Console>;
    small: Buffer<2>;
}

machine Main::main(&mut self) reaches Console {
    transition { _ -> step(1) }

    state step(&mut self, count: i32) {
        self.console.exit_process(0);
    }
}
